// core:mem — the allocator interface, the rules every allocator keeps, and the
// byte helpers an allocator written in Dado is made of.
//
//     import "core:mem"
//     import "core:mem/libc"
//
//     (mem.Proc proc, rawptr data) a = libc.allocator()
//     rawptr p = a.proc(a.data, mem.AllocatorMode.Alloc, 64, 16, nil, 0)
//     a.proc(a.data, mem.AllocatorMode.Free, 0, 0, p, 64)
//
// An allocator is expressible with what the language already
// has and **needs no compiler support to define**. This package and its
// siblings (`mem/libc`, `mem/bump`, `mem/arena`, `mem/slab`, `mem/gc`) are that
// claim, cashed: nothing below is privileged, nothing below asks the compiler
// for anything, and a user writes an allocator exactly the way these do.
//
// What it uses, and all it uses: function values in slots,
// `rawptr`, enums, and ordinary functions. `#new`, `#make`,
// `#resize`, `#delete` and `#copy` are the *verbs* the compiler lowers
// through this interface; they are not what the interface is made of.
//
// ## Allocation is `using`, not a parameter
//
// **A library function does not take an allocator.** It allocates on
// `#default`, and its doc comment says who ends what it answers ("the caller
// `#delete`s it", "end it with `destroy`"). A caller who wants the memory from
// an arena, a slab or a scratch frame says so once, around the call:
//
//     using arena.allocator(&scratch):
//         ref []char8 line = strings.concat(a, b)
//
// An allocator parameter is the exception, and its doc comment says why: a
// value that captures an allocator for its whole life and outlives the `using`
// it was made in, a process-lifetime answer deliberately on `#heap`, or an
// allocator package itself (these, whose subject *is* the allocator). Where an
// allocator must be spelled, a signature takes `Allocator` (the trait, as a
// binder) where only implementers are passed, else the two words
// `(mem.Proc proc, rawptr data)`, which a rung such as `#default` also is.
//
// ## What every allocator answers
//
// * **`nil` is failure**, and the only failure: the interface has no codes.
// * **Allocation is zeroed**, `Alloc` whole and `Resize` from the old size
//   up, because `i32 a` is zero and a `T` from an allocator that was not
//   would make that rule depend on where the storage came from.
// * **A zero-size allocation answers a distinct, non-nil pointer that may not
//   be dereferenced**: distinct from every other live block, so a zero-length
//   run is a real handle, and non-nil, so it does not read as failure. Freeing
//   it with `old_size` 0 is valid. C's `malloc(0)` may answer NULL, so an
//   allocator over C asks for one byte, as the compiler's own root heap does
//   (`calloc(1, size ? size : 1)`).
// * **The alignment asked for, up to `MAX_ALIGN`.** Above it an allocator may
//   refuse (`nil`) rather than under-align.
//
// **Every target**: freestanding (`#NONE`), the web, and hosted (Linux, macOS
// and Windows) alike. Nothing here reaches a C library.
//
// **What raises into a script.** `align_up`, promised by the `#assert_raises`
// above it: it is arithmetic. The interface, `zero`, `copy` and
// `resize_by_copy` are made of `rawptr`s, and a script has no addresses.
//
// **Thread-safety.** No package state. Whether one allocator may be called
// from two threads is that allocator's to say.
package mem

// ── The four modes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// What an allocator is asked to do: the second argument of every call.
//
// Odin's eight, minus four. `Alloc_Non_Zeroed` and
// `Resize_Non_Zeroed` are a boolean wearing a mode and allocation always zeroes;
// `Query_Features` and `Query_Info` exist so generic code can ask an allocator
// what it implements, and trying and getting `nil` answers the same question.
//
// The procedure returns the block for `Alloc` and `Resize` — `nil` on failure —
// and `nil` for `Free` and `FreeAll`. The prelude also names this type
// `AllocatorMode`, as `i32`; the library spells it `mem.AllocatorMode`.
enum AllocatorMode: (Alloc, Resize, Free, FreeAll)

// ── The interface ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The allocator procedure: the first of the two words an allocator is. Its
// parameters, which a function type has no place to name:
//
//     data      the allocator's own state, handed back on every call
//     mode      which of the four operations this call is
//     size      how many bytes are wanted (`Alloc`, `Resize`)
//     align     what the block must be aligned to
//     old       the block being resized or freed, `nil` otherwise
//     old_size  how large that block was
//
// `align` is in the signature because `malloc` returns memory aligned for
// anything and a bump arena hands out offsets into its own block and must
// align them itself. It is a **parameter**, never something an allocator
// computes: the caller knows the type, the allocator knows only bytes.
//
// A name for the shape and not a new type: `(mem.Proc proc, rawptr data)` and
// the shape written out are one type, so either spelling takes `#default`,
// `libc.allocator()` and every other pair of words.
type Proc: rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64)

// **An allocator, as a trait whose one required method answers its two
// words**: `data()` hands back the procedure and the allocator's own state (an
// arena's cursor, a pool's free list), which is handed back on every call and
// is what lets one procedure serve many arenas. A declaration that takes
// `Allocator` on is accepted wherever an allocator is asked, the compiler
// calling its `data()` there; a program that holds the two words themselves
// writes `(mem.Proc proc, rawptr data)`.
//
// > A bump arena is a procedure that advances a cursor for `Alloc`, ignores
// > `Free`, and resets for `FreeAll`, plus a `data` pointing at its block.
trait Allocator:
    (Proc proc, rawptr data) data(self)

    // Whether the allocator has been given a procedure. Two nil words are
    // what `#default` holds on a freestanding target — so this is the check a
    // program written for both targets makes. Of two words held as such, ask
    // `a.proc != nil` directly.
    bool ok(self):
        return self.data().proc != nil

// ── Alignment ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The alignment every shipped allocator hands out: worst case, always correct,
// and a few bytes wasted.
//
// The compiler computes this exactly, with an `offsetof(struct { char c;
// T t; }, t)` probe over a union of `long double`, `void *` and `long long` —
// a spelling every C standard answers, C99's included. **This constant
// is not that.** It is what an allocator written in Dado has to hand-build when
// it wants to align something on its own account, and 16 is the value that
// probe yields on every target Dado targets today.
//
// It is a ceiling rather than a measurement, and that is the honest reading:
// over-aligning is always correct and costs padding, under-aligning is a
// fault. `align` travels in the signature so precision can arrive later
// without an ABI break.
const u64 MAX_ALIGN = 16

// `n` rounded up to a multiple of `align`. `align` must be a power of two,
// which every alignment is; `0` and `1` both mean "no constraint".
#assert_raises
u64 align_up(u64 n, u64 align):
    if align <= 1:
        return n
    u64 rem = n % align
    if rem == 0:
        return n
    return n + (align - rem)

// ── Bytes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Zero the `n` bytes at `p`: how an allocator keeps "allocation is zeroed"
// over storage it reuses. A nil `p` or a zero `n` does nothing.
void zero(rawptr p, u64 n):
    if p == nil || n == 0:
        return
    i64 total = i64(n)
    Bytes b = Bytes(p)
    []u8 span = b[0..<total]
    for i in i64(0)..<total:
        span[#no_bounds i] = 0
    return

// Copy `n` bytes from `src` to `dst`, front to back: correct when the two do
// not overlap, and when `dst` is below `src` (a move downward, which is the
// one an arena compacting towards a mark makes). A move upward over itself is
// not this. A nil pointer or a zero `n` does nothing.
void copy(rawptr dst, rawptr src, u64 n):
    if dst == nil || src == nil || n == 0 || dst == src:
        return
    i64 total = i64(n)
    Bytes bs = Bytes(src)
    Bytes bd = Bytes(dst)
    []u8 from = bs[0..<total]
    []u8 into = bd[0..<total]
    for i in i64(0)..<total:
        into[#no_bounds i] = from[#no_bounds i]
    return

// The block, as the run of bytes it is: a `^u8` is what a `rawptr` converts to
// for free, and a window taken over it (`b[0..<n]`) is a `[]u8` of a written
// length. `zero` and `copy` are written with it.
type Bytes: ^u8

// ── The fallback `Resize` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `Resize` by allocating, copying and freeing, through `a`: what an allocator
// with nothing clever to do calls for that mode, rather than reporting it
// unimplemented and pushing a fallback onto the caller. A nil `old` is an
// `Alloc`; a failed allocation answers `nil` and leaves `old` untouched and
// still the caller's.
//
// `realloc` is genuinely privileged — it can grow a block **in place** when the
// heap has adjacent space — but only from *inside* the allocator, which is why
// `Resize` is a mode rather than something the caller synthesizes. There is
// deliberately **no caller-side fallback**: a hidden three-call path inside
// `#resize` would make "did my data move?" unpredictable, and the whole claim
// is that every disturbance is a call you can see.
rawptr resize_by_copy((Proc proc, rawptr data) a, rawptr old, u64 old_size, u64 nbytes, u64 align):
    rawptr fresh = a.proc(a.data, AllocatorMode.Alloc, nbytes, align, nil, 0)
    if old == nil || fresh == nil:
        return fresh
    u64 keep = old_size
    if nbytes < keep: keep = nbytes
    copy(fresh, old, keep)
    _ = a.proc(a.data, AllocatorMode.Free, 0, 0, old, old_size)
    return fresh
